WEBVTT

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:05.070
JavaScript and TypeScript applications still need runtime validation because external data

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.017
arrives at runtime.

00:00:06.017 --> 00:00:08.400
TypeScript can help developers reason about code,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.140
but it does not prove that a request body, query parameter, route parameter, header,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.050
cookie, or webhook payload matches the expected shape.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:19.445
JSON parsing only proves that data is parseable, not that it is safe, authorized,

00:00:19.445 --> 00:00:20.790
complete, or meaningful.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:24.885
Schema validation gives teams a clear way to define expected fields, types, formats,

00:00:24.885 --> 00:00:26.360
ranges, and constraints.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.240
It can reject unknown fields, normalize allowed values,

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.685
and keep invalid data away from business logic.

00:00:31.685 --> 00:00:36.240
This is especially important in APIs where clients may send extra fields, nested objects,

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.350
unexpected arrays, or values that look harmless until they reach a database query,

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.655
file operation, or downstream service.

00:00:42.655 --> 00:00:45.350
Type confusion and mass assignment are common design risks.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:49.570
Type confusion happens when code assumes data has one type or shape but receives something

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:49.950
else.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.780
Mass assignment happens when user-controlled fields can set properties that should not be

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:54.800
user-controlled,

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.840
such as roles, ownership, approval status, or internal flags.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.865
Validation should define both what is allowed and what must never be accepted from the

00:01:01.865 --> 00:01:02.380
client.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:06.865
Validation should happen before data reaches business logic, databases, files, commands,

00:01:06.865 --> 00:01:08.190
or downstream services.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.608
Error responses should be useful but not revealing.

00:01:10.608 --> 00:01:14.590
A client can be told that input failed validation without receiving stack traces,

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:18.250
internal schema details, database messages, or implementation paths.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:22.630
Good validation improves security, reliability, and developer clarity at the same time.
